WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.938
Unit 3 is Step 2, the
public exposure inventory.

2
00:00:03.938 --> 00:00:07.696
Learn's page, Secure your Azure
DDoS Protection deployment,

3
00:00:07.696 --> 00:00:12.145
recommends an inventory of every
public I P and its protection status,

4
00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:15.213
kept with Resource Graph,
and a quarterly review.

5
00:00:15.213 --> 00:00:18.977
The guide's four queries list
public I Ps with their DDoS state,

6
00:00:18.977 --> 00:00:23.921
Front Door profiles, application gateways
with their web application firewall

7
00:00:23.921 --> 00:00:28.201
policy, and pass resources whose
public network access isn't disabled.

8
00:00:28.201 --> 00:00:31.875
The first is built from Learn's
starter Resource Graph query.

9
00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:35.308
The register then adds what
the queries can't: the owner,

10
00:00:35.308 --> 00:00:39.047
the D N S names and certificates
that point at each exposure,

11
00:00:39.047 --> 00:00:42.175
and whether a web application
firewall covers it.

12
00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:43.338
Two cautions.

13
00:00:43.338 --> 00:00:47.598
The public network access query is
a starting list, not a complete one,

14
00:00:47.598 --> 00:00:50.962
because the property's name
and values vary by service.

15
00:00:50.962 --> 00:00:54.039
And the register is a map
of the attack surface:

16
00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:57.980
it stays inside the boundary,
never in a public repository.
